// Practice quiz: Singleton and factory patterns
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/singleton-and-factory-patterns

::o1 recognize::Which combination marks a classic singleton? {
=A private constructor and a static method that returns the one shared instance#Correct. Nobody else can create another instance.
~A public constructor and a static counter#Not quite. Anyone could still create more instances.
~An abstract class with one subclass#Not quite. Abstract classes are about subclassing, not one instance.
~A record with one component#Not quite. Records can be created freely.
}

::o1 hidden global state::Why is a singleton holding mutable settings risky? {
=Any code can change it, so one test or feature can silently affect another#Correct. It is global state reached from anywhere without appearing in parameters.
~Singletons cannot hold fields#Not quite. They can, which is the source of the risk.
~It is always slower than a normal object#Not quite. Speed is not the main concern.
~It can only be used once#Not quite. It can be used many times; that is the point.
}

::o2 static factory::What does a static factory method such as Shape.of(kind) let the class hide? {
=Which concrete class is created and how it is built#Correct. Callers ask for what they want and receive the supertype.
~That an object is being created at all#Not quite. Callers still receive a new or cached object.
~The return type#Not quite. The return type is declared in the signature.
~Every method of the returned object#Not quite. Callers use the returned object's public methods.
}

::o2 factory benefit::Which is a real benefit of a static factory method over a public constructor? {
=It has a descriptive name and may return a cached object or a subclass#Correct. A constructor must always create a new object of exactly its own class.
~It removes the need for any constructor in the class#Not quite. The class still has a constructor, often private.
~It makes the object immutable#Not quite. Immutability depends on the class's fields.
~It works without the class being loaded#Not quite. The class is loaded to run any static method.
}

::o3 pass it in::A PriceService needs a TaxTable. Which design avoids hidden global state? {
=Pass the TaxTable to PriceService's constructor#Correct. The dependency is visible, and a test can pass a different table.
~Call TaxTable.getInstance() inside every method#Not quite. That hides the dependency inside the class.
~Copy the TaxTable values into PriceService constants#Not quite. Changes would then need code edits.
~Make PriceService a singleton too#Not quite. That adds more global state.
}

::o3 testing::Why is a class that calls a singleton's getInstance() hard to unit test? {
=The test cannot easily give it a different instance#Correct. The dependency is fetched internally rather than supplied.
~getInstance() cannot be called from tests#Not quite. Tests can call it, but they get the same shared object.
~Singletons are never thread-safe#Not quite. That is a separate issue.
~The class will not compile in a test#Not quite. It compiles; isolating it is what is hard.
}
